<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    The layers docker's rows: a layer, a folder header, and what a drag over
    either of them says it would do.

    Lifted out of MainWindow.axaml whole rather than split. The comment below
    already records why these live in one place — three inline copies drifted
    apart once — and leaving half of them behind in the window would have been
    that mistake with an extra file. It moved because the window is on a
    ratchet: B346 added the drop styles, the file may shrink but not grow, and
    the honest answer to "it has to grow" is to take something out of it.
  -->

  <!--
    A row is as tall as its icon tile and no taller.

    `Padding="2,0"` rather than `2`: the horizontal 2 keeps the first icon off
    the edge, the vertical 0 is the density. The tile is already 26 with the
    glyph centred in it, so vertical padding was buying nothing but height —
    4 px a row, which is a whole extra row every eight layers.

    Declared here rather than on each template, which is also why the three
    inline copies are gone. They were identical by luck: the layers docker,
    the group row and the timeline's layer column each wrote their own, so a
    change to one silently split the lists that are meant to line up.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Border.layerRow">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2,0" />
  </Style>
  <!--
    A row in a Ctrl/Shift multi-selection (B400): a bar on the left edge and a
    fill between hover and active — under the pointer < in the selection <
    where the brush lands, each a stronger statement than the last.

    HERE, between the base style above and `.active` below, and the order is
    the whole fix. Avalonia does not rank selectors by specificity: of two
    styles at the same level, the later one wins. This used to sit in
    Controls.axaml, which AppStyles includes BEFORE this file, so the base
    `Border.layerRow` above reset every selected row to Transparent. From the
    move of these styles here (2026-08-29) until B400, a multi-selection drew
    nothing at all — five weeks in which every Ctrl/Shift fix worked in the
    model and was reported by the owner as still broken.
    `SelectedRowVisibilityTests` reads the drawn brush, not the class.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Border.layerRow.selected">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SelectionWeakBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource SelectionBar}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The layer being drawn on is "on": the one flat fill every "on" takes (Q203).
       Later than `.selected`, so a row that is both reads as active — and keeps
       the bar, since the active layer is always in the selection. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.layerRow.active">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ActiveFillBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.layerRow.hiddenLayer">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.35" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Cyan, not the warning amber: locked is a STATE the artist chose, not
       a problem the app is reporting, and amber made every locked layer read
       as something going wrong. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.layerRow.lockedLayer">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentCyanBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.groupRow.lockedLayer">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentCyanBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
  </Style>
  <!--
    A folder's colour, as a wash across the whole row (Q226): strongly on the
    folder's own header, faintly on every layer inside it, so a folder reads as
    one band rather than as a four-pixel bar beside one of its rows. A subfolder
    shows its parent's colour until it is given its own.

    On the row's CONTAINER, not on the row. The row's Background belongs to the
    styles above — transparent, selected, active — and those fills are
    translucent white, so they have to be drawn OVER the wash to stay the
    strongest thing on the row; a wash bound onto the row itself would replace
    them, which is the B400 failure with a colour on it. It also keeps the
    window's markup, which is on a ratchet, out of it.

    A folder header used to carry an opaque grey to set it apart from a layer.
    The wash does that now, and the grey would have hidden it.
  -->
  <Style Selector="ItemsControl#LayerList > ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ReflectionBinding TintBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusControl}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.groupRow">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2,0" />
  </Style>
  <!-- A folder picked on its header: drawn as a selected layer row is (B400),
       since several can be picked and each is "in the selection". After the
       base groupRow style above, for the reason `.layerRow.selected` gives. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.groupRow.selected">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SelectionWeakBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource SelectionBar}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.groupRow.hiddenLayer">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.35" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Where a dragged row or folder would land. A LINE on the edge it would
       land against, because that is the one reading with no ambiguity: a
       highlighted row says "this row is involved" and leaves which side to
       guess at. Accent rather than the selection brush, so it cannot be
       mistaken for the row already being selected. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.dropAbove">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentCoralBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,2,0,0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.dropBelow">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentCoralBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,2" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Into a folder is the one drop that is not an edge, so it is the one
       that lights the row instead of drawing a line beside it. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.groupRow.dropInto">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SelectionBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentCoralBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
